WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.940
It almost seems to be an unwritten Hollywood
rule that for every amazing, well-crafted

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:09.560
piece of cinema released in any given year,
there must be at least one completely misguided,

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:13.040
poorly executed, lazily written slog of a
film.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.640
2019 is no exception - and these are the worst
movies of the bunch.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:22.000
Keanu Reeves is as in demand as ever, and
has even cemented his action star bona fides

00:00:22.019 --> 00:00:24.960
relatively late in his career thanks to the
John Wick series.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.199
Over the years, he's only appeared in a handful
of sci-fi features, so we were psyched to

00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:33.070
see his return to the world of trippy sci-fi
weirdness with Replicas.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:35.500
Then the reviews started to come in.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:40.020
According to critics, the film was a complete
mess, plagued by a clumsy script written in

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.150
service of an absurd storyline.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:47.070
Even worse for an action star of Reeves' caliber,
it also featured unexciting action sequences

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.370
and plot holes you could drive a semi through.

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:53.930
The Guardian's Charles Bramesco offered up
a Roger Ebert-esque review that took everyone

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:55.120
but Reeves to task:

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:59.730
"If only anyone else involved could muster
a fraction of the consummate professionalism

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:04.589
that Reeves brings to his dead-serious intonation
of the word 'algorithm,' this DOA science

00:01:04.589 --> 00:01:06.409
project might have had a pulse."

00:01:06.409 --> 00:01:08.030
All we can say to that review is:

00:01:15.539 --> 00:01:20.150
for its last-minute reveal: just before the
credits rolled, audiences realized that Split

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:24.980
was a stealth sequel to Shyamalan's amazing
2000 film Unbreakable.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:29.360
Shyamalan brought it all together for the
third film in the sudden trilogy, Glass, which

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.080
promised us a superpowered throwdown for the
ages.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:37.770
Typical of many of Shyamalan's movies, a vocal
minority of critics found it to be a misunderstood

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:38.790
masterpiece.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:43.570
Everyone else, however, simply found it to
be poorly written, ploddingly paced, and boring.

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:48.400
Critic Mahmoud Mahdy of Film Gamed summed
up the film with one harsh sentence in his

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:49.400
review:

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:53.810
"A wagon carrying dramatic garbage advancing
on rails at a constant pace to reach a destination

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:55.820
where nobody ever wanted to go."

00:01:55.820 --> 00:02:00.530
Serenity, starring Matthew McConaughey as
a fishing boat captain and Anne Hathaway as

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:05.280
an ex-wife who shows up seeking protection
from her new abusive husband, is a seedy murder

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:06.690
mystery with a twist.

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:10.860
The problem, unfortunately, is that the mystery
isn't so tough to parse out, and the twist

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.630
is absolutely ridiculous.

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:16.470
In case you think we're being hyperbolic,
consider the words of the great critic Rex

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:19.860
Reed, who has seen a bad movie or two in his
distinguished career.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:23.470
Writing for the Observer around the movie's
release in January, Reed argued:

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:26.910
"Serenity already qualifies as the worst film
of 2019.

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:31.380
Both moronically written and directed with
shocking, amateurish ineptitude by Stephen

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.070
Knight, it's a pointless bomb."

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:35.740
Say what you will about the movie, but that
sure doesn't sound...

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:38.500
"Alright, alright, alright."

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:43.900
Gina Rodriguez is probably most familiar to
audiences by way of her starring role on the

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:49.060
CW's romantic comedy series Jane the Virgin,
so it's a little unclear why Miss Bala would

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:50.940
attempt to make her over as an action star.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:56.010
A remake of the 2011 Spanish film of the same
name, Miss Bala stars Rodriguez as a makeup

00:02:56.010 --> 00:03:00.690
artist who gets caught up in an extremely
convoluted cross-border drug trafficking plot

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:03.580
while attempting to help a friend win a beauty
contest.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:08.510
While the film itself was roundly panned,
critics made sure to give Rodriguez some credit.

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:12.980
Bilge Ebiri writing at the Rolling Stone manage
to ding the film and praise Rodriguez in the

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:13.980
same review:

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:18.380
"This Hollywoodized remake is mostly generic
and uninspired, but it also proves that Rodriguez

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:20.230
has the makings of a true movie star.

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:24.460
It’ll be exciting to see what she does next
[…] so long as it’s not a sequel to this."

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:29.940
Another year, another entry in the seemingly
unkillable Madea series.

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:33.950
Every new installment brings two different
sets of lines around the block: fans who can't

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:37.840
wait to catch the latest outing, and critics
who can't wait to take their shots.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:43.300
Tyler Perry's A Madea Family Funeral was certainly
no different, garnering $72 million at the

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:48.140
domestic box office while reliably bringing
out maximum snark in virtually ever reviewer

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:49.790
tasked with sitting through it.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:52.910
Frank Scheck of The Hollywood Reporter definitely
didn't hold any punches:

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:56.670
"Considering how well the character has served
him, Perry certainly doesn't return the favor

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:01.920
in this graceless installment combining raucous
comedy and turgid melodrama to undigestible

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.390
effect…This film, like almost all of his
others, has a tossed-off quality, giving the

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:08.700
impression it was written and shot over a
long weekend."

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:13.780
John Travolta has accomplished many things
in his career: he helped to cement the popularity

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:18.880
of disco, assisted Quentin Tarantino in reinventing
the crime film, and traded faces with Nicolas

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:20.450
Cage, to name just a few.

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:25.320
But 2019 saw him notch perhaps his most dubious
achievement: fielding a picture which scored

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:29.220
a big fat goose egg on Rotten Tomatoes for
the third time in two years.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:34.630
His misbegotten biopic Gotti and woefully
inept speedboat-racing thriller Speed Kills

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:38.250
were considered by many to be the worst films
of 2018.

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:42.040
Trading Paint, the story of father-and-son
race car drivers pitted against each other

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:46.550
by an unscrupulous rival, is shaping up to
be a strong contender for this year's title.

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:52.160
The 11 critics who bothered to review it trashed
it mercilessly, none more so than Johnny Oleksinski

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:53.290
of the New York Post:

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:57.400
"The races of “Trading Paint”... are as
exciting as a Ford Taurus trying to parallel

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:58.400
park."

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:02.269
The silver lining here: a losing streak this
egregious usually means there's nowhere to

00:05:02.269 --> 00:05:03.500
go but up.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:09.570
Director Guillermo del Toro's Hellboy adaptation
from 2004, and its 2008 sequel The Golden

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:13.910
Army were fine adaptations of the famous comic
book character, and the two movies have plenty

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:15.120
of fans to this day.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:19.470
That's largely thanks to the director's unabashed
love for the material, as well as a lead performance

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:22.660
from Ron Perlman in a role he was born to
play.

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:23.790
"Code red."

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:25.480
"Hey, hey, hey.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:27.730
They're playing our song.

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:29.190
Let's go fight some monsters."

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:34.020
A third del Toro-directed installment was
rumored for years, but fell apart due to the

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:37.769
producers' desire to go the reboot route,
much to the disappointment of Perlman and

00:05:37.769 --> 00:05:38.800
many fans.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:42.710
But with Stranger Things' David Harbour in
the lead, The Descent's Neil Marshall in the

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:47.549
director's chair, and a hard R rating, the
2019 edition of Hellboy looked promising.

00:05:47.549 --> 00:05:50.650
That is, right up until it landed in theaters
with a resounding thud.

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:54.070
While there was plenty of praise for Harbour's
take on the character, critics found it to

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:56.460
be a performance in service of a terrible
film.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:59.970
Spencer Perry at Coming Soon let it fly in
a brutal review:

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:03.790
"The new Hellboy wouldn’t be so bad if it
wasn’t so boring and ugly to look at.

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:07.370
Moments that happen just for the sake of moving
the plot could be forgiven, but its garish

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:12.220
visuals hinder it and its plodding plot doesn’t
come together in any satisfying way."

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:18.570
At first blush, a gender-flipped take on the
1988 comedy classic Dirty Rotten Scoundrels,

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:22.750
which featured Michael Caine and Steve Martin
as dueling con men vying over the territory

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:26.470
of a seaside resort, seemed like a premise
with a lot of potential.

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:30.770
The Hustle also had a lot going for it: Anne
Hathaway and Rebel Wilson in the lead roles,

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:33.800
along with Veep veteran Chris Addison in the
director's chair.

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:37.380
But something must have gone wrong between
the conception and execution, because The

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:41.850
Hustle debuted to some of the harshest critical
thrashings of the year thus far.

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:45.700
Onetime Roger Ebert cohort Richard Roeper
channeled his old friend in his blistering

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:47.079
review for the Chicago Sun-Times:

00:06:47.079 --> 00:06:48.820
"So predictable.

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:50.320
Just like everything in this movie.

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:54.500
If you don’t see the long con coming in
this story, either you’re not paying attention

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:55.500
or … No.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.840
That’s the only possible explanation.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:01.290
Your mind wandered to thoughts of better movies
playing elsewhere, perhaps right next door

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:02.300
to this debacle."

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:06.329
Meanwhile, The Detroit News' Adam Graham perhaps
nailed the film the hardest:

00:07:06.329 --> 00:07:07.570
"Who's conning who here?

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:11.260
In The Hustle, everyone's a sucker, most of
all the audience."

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:17.060
The Intruder is a bit of an anomaly: a film
featuring a widely-praised performance from

00:07:17.079 --> 00:07:21.150
a veteran actor that was nevertheless mercilessly
dragged by critics.

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:25.940
The performance in question comes from square-jawed,
generally nice Dennis Quaid as Charlie Peck,

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:30.910
a seemingly nice older dude who sells his
family home to a young couple only to creepily

00:07:30.910 --> 00:07:33.390
insinuate himself into their lives.

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:37.070
Quaid's against-type, wildly over-the-top
performance might have been remembered as

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:38.769
one for the ages by thriller aficionados.

00:07:38.769 --> 00:07:42.130
If only it had been in the service of a better
film.

00:07:42.130 --> 00:07:46.320
Film Frenzy's Matt Brunson took particular
issue with how poorly the film set up its

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:47.320
protagonists:

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:51.069
"If the protagonists in this film were any
more dense, they would only exist as a thick

00:07:51.069 --> 00:07:52.069
fog."

00:07:52.069 --> 00:07:56.720
Nearly every negative review similarly lamented
the film's utter predictability and ham-fisted

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:01.120
jump scares while admitting that seeing Quaid
play a complete psycho was pretty fun.

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:03.950
The Critical Movie Critics' Frank Ochieng
said:

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:08.890
"Unimaginative and clumsy, this generic groan-at-home
sideshow’s only genuine appeal is watching

00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:14.080
veteran screen star Quaid overly ham it up
as the intruding, nutty nemesis hellbent on

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:15.900
reclaiming his territory."

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:20.820
Poms billed itself as an "uplifting comedy"
about a group of women who start up a cheerleading

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:25.420
squad in their retirement home, and with a
premise like that, casting is everything.

00:08:25.420 --> 00:08:29.999
Fortunately, screen legends Diane Keaton,
Pam Grier, and Rhea Perlman were all on board,

00:08:29.999 --> 00:08:32.700
and the reviews certainly had no problems
with the cast.

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:36.210
Instead, the critics' issues were with virtually
everything else about the film.

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:40.439
It probably didn't help that Poms was the
debut feature for both director Zara Hayes

00:08:40.439 --> 00:08:42.670
and screenwriter Shane Atkinson.

00:08:42.670 --> 00:08:46.770
Critics took the movie to task for everything
from wasting its brilliant cast to disrespecting

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:48.640
the age group it was meant to uplift.

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:52.999
Ella Taylor at NPR pointed out a particularly
grim plot point in her review of the film:

00:08:52.999 --> 00:08:57.050
"... By hitching Martha's imminent death to
the innocuous frivolity of a comic cheerleading

00:08:57.050 --> 00:09:01.240
plot, Hayes and screenwriter Shane Atkinson
take on responsibilities they can't follow

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:04.590
through on without getting into a tonal and
thematic tangle…

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:09.350
As a fable of end-of-life empowerment, the
movie has all the existential heft of an extended

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:10.990
L'Oreal commercial."

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:14.731
Rex Reed of the Observer succinctly pointed
out the biggest problem in the film, and it's

00:09:14.731 --> 00:09:16.510
not any of the talented cast:

00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:21.309
"It’s the witless script by Shane Atkinson
and the petrified direction by Zara Hayes

00:09:21.309 --> 00:09:23.860
that lands everyone in traction."

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:28.970
The films of the Conjuring universe have certainly
made their share of box office bank, but critically,

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:30.660
they've been a bit of a mixed bag.

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:35.360
In just the last couple years, reviewers have
swung from hot, in the case of 2019's Annabelle

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:38.560
Comes Home, to ice cold for 2018's The Nun.

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:42.970
Unfortunately, the response to The Curse of
La Llorona, based on a creepy bit of Mexican

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:47.510
folklore that seemed ripe for cinematic exploration,
came down firmly on the "cold" side of that

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:48.510
equation.

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:51.930
Focusing on a social worker who incurs the
wrath of a supernatural entity that preys

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:57.340
on children, Llorona turned critics off with
its extreme reliance on formulaic scares.

00:09:57.340 --> 00:10:02.170
Making things worse, some felt it failed to
do justice to the true creepiness of the legend

00:10:02.170 --> 00:10:03.300
on which it's based.

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:05.230
According to Alternate Ending's Tim Brayton:

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:09.850
"It can only barely get jump scares right;
every single one of them for the film's first

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:14.450
hour is set-up, staged, and timed exactly
the same way, so that they start to feel less

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:19.020
like visceral sucker punches and more like
a baffling and unfunny running gag."

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:23.080
But National Post's Chris Knight put the final
nail in La Llorona's coffin:

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:26.870
"Some horror films, like last year's Hereditary,
are flat-out horrifying.

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:31.580
Others, such as the recent Halloween or Pet
Sematary reboots, are legitimately frightening.

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:34.280
The Curse of La Llorona… is neither of these.

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:36.220
The best you could say is it's startling."

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:41.499
Dark Phoenix was supposed to be a lot of things:
an epic sendoff to the heroes of the Fox-produced

00:10:41.499 --> 00:10:46.010
X-Men series, a proper film adaptation of
one of the greatest Marvel storylines of all

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:47.990
time, and, ideally, a hit movie.

00:10:47.990 --> 00:10:51.790
But because of an extensive reworking of its
third act and the inexperience of first-time

00:10:51.790 --> 00:10:55.780
director Simon Kinberg, it ended up being
exactly none of those things.

00:10:55.780 --> 00:11:00.160
Matthew Lickona of the San Diego Reader seemed
nearly disappointed when he wrote his review:

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:04.740
"It didn’t have to end this way - in such
thoroughly standard smash ‘em up fashion,

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:09.780
with minor heroes dutifully duking it out
with faceless hordes for punchy-power bolt

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:14.640
minute after punchy-power-bolt minute until
the mayhem quotient has been met and the principals

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:17.990
can finally square off for their climactic
lightshow.

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:22.210
More reviews, like Michael Calleri's of the
Niagara Gazette seemed to echo the melancholy:

00:11:22.210 --> 00:11:25.100
"Much of the cast actually looks exhausted.

00:11:25.100 --> 00:11:28.439
Some of them seem as if a pall of ennui had
settled over their persona.

00:11:28.439 --> 00:11:31.100
Dark Phoenix is a dull money grab and nothing
more."

00:11:32.140 --> 00:11:36.580
Fielding a fourth installment in the Men in
Black series seven years after the previous

00:11:36.590 --> 00:11:40.260
installment, without franchise stars Will
Smith and Tommy Lee Jones, was always a bit

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:41.580
of a risky proposition.

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:46.020
Still, Men in Black: International had two
charismatic leads in Chris Hemsworth and Tessa

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:50.200
Thompson who had already proven their amazing
screen chemistry with Thor: Ragnarok.

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:55.020
"I am Thor, Son of Odin, I need to get back
to Asgard."

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:58.060
"Many apologies, your majesty."

00:11:58.060 --> 00:12:02.090
Unfortunately, it takes more than just a couple
of funny and talented actors to make a good

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:03.090
movie.

00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:06.830
Even with the participation of Iron Man scribes
Art Marcum and Matt Holloway and Friday director

00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:11.330
F. Gary Gray, Men in Black: International
fell woefully short of past installments of

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:12.330
the franchise.

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:16.090
Joe Morgenstern of The Wall Street Journal
barely seemed willing to review the film at

00:12:16.090 --> 00:12:17.090
all:

00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:19.460
"No need to huff and puff about the idiocy
of 'Men in Black: International.'

00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:24.400
It is what it is, an industrial product recycled
from the remnants of an exhausted franchise

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:29.150
and aimed at a young audience that may not
know or care what a joy the original was."

00:12:29.150 --> 00:12:33.360
While many reviewers had praise for Hemsworth
and Thompson's comedic stylings, the movie

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:35.059
as a whole was roundly trashed.

00:12:35.059 --> 00:12:40.080
Oliver Jones at The Observer spared some brief
praise for Kumail Nanjiani's alien character

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:42.400
Pawny before blasting away at the film:

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:46.470
"Certainly, Pawny is mildly cute and, wielding
a tiny sword that looks like it might struggle

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:48.450
to mince garlic, mostly harmless.

00:12:48.450 --> 00:12:52.490
Those are also the nicest things you can say
about the movie he is in, a largely toothless

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:56.640
and wit-free reboot that empties the last
of the originality and vivacity that has been

00:12:56.640 --> 00:13:00.580
steadily leaking out of the franchise with
each subsequent installment.

00:13:00.820 --> 00:13:06.300
On paper, it sounded Shaft-tastic: Richard
Roundtree reprising his role as John Shaft

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:12.451
from the iconic 1971 original; Samuel L. Jackson
returning as his son, John Shaft, Jr., from

00:13:12.451 --> 00:13:16.210
the well-received 2000 sequel; and Jessie
Usher as J.J.

00:13:16.210 --> 00:13:19.200
Shaft, the latest in the line of famous badasses.

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:23.700
Unfortunately, it didn't live up to any of
its potential, and critics generally agreed

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:26.999
that Shaft 2019 was a throwback in all the
wrong ways.

00:13:26.999 --> 00:13:31.330
Peter Travers of Rolling Stone called the
film shamelessly regressive in his review:

00:13:31.330 --> 00:13:33.249
"Rough justice is back to stay.

00:13:33.249 --> 00:13:35.160
Women are out of the picture, except for sex.

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:39.660
Dinosaurs again walk the earth with misogynistic
and homophobic impunity.

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:41.050
These are the laughs, folks.

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:43.590
Don’t be surprised if they stick in your
throat."

00:13:43.590 --> 00:13:48.280
RogerEbert.com's Odie Henderson was arguably
even harsher on the film, saying:

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:53.090
"This movie is 'ruin your childhood' bad,
right down to the hideous auto-tuned end credits

00:13:53.090 --> 00:13:56.780
song they chose to use instead of the original
'Theme From Shaft.'"

00:13:56.780 --> 00:14:00.240
But Jason Bailey of Flavorwire summed it up
the best, writing:

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:04.429
"If you know one thing about John Shaft’s
history and legacy, [the film is] downright

00:14:04.429 --> 00:14:05.380
depressing"

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:10.929
French director Luc Besson has made a career
out of making ultra-stylized flicks featuring

00:14:10.929 --> 00:14:16.650
extremely formidable women, from 1990's Nikita
to 1997's The Fifth Element to 2014's Lucy.

00:14:16.650 --> 00:14:21.950
For his latest exploration of this theme,
he recruited Russian supermodel Sasha Luss

00:14:21.950 --> 00:14:25.820
for the title role in Anna, the story of an
everyday woman who happens to look like a

00:14:25.820 --> 00:14:29.900
Russian supermodel and somehow becomes a cold-blooded
killer for the KGB.

00:14:29.900 --> 00:14:34.340
If it all sounds a little familiar, critics
agreed - many found the film so highly reminiscent

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:37.510
of Besson's previous work that it bordered
on self-parody.

00:14:37.510 --> 00:14:41.929
This may have been fine if the picture were
serviceably made, but most observers found

00:14:41.929 --> 00:14:46.429
Besson to be stuck firmly in neutral with
Anna, a sparsely-plotted film with a distinct

00:14:46.429 --> 00:14:47.739
lack of imagination.

00:14:47.739 --> 00:14:49.650
The Guardian's Benjamin Lee said that:

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:54.310
"What ultimately sinks the film is its overwhelming
blandness, from the lack of creativity employed

00:14:54.310 --> 00:14:59.130
in Anna’s many kills to Besson’s inability
to choreograph a pulse-racing action scene,

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:02.809
and the script’s belaboured attempts to
jump back and forward in time to wrongfoot

00:15:02.809 --> 00:15:05.320
us only highlight how little it brings to
the table."

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:10.559
Meanwhile, Peter Sobczynski of RogerEbert.com
critiqued the film for not living up to Besson's

00:15:10.559 --> 00:15:11.559
previous works:

00:15:11.559 --> 00:15:15.499
"...Even long-standing fans of his will find
it hard to muster much enthusiasm for this

00:15:15.499 --> 00:15:18.429
startlingly lazy bit of by-the-numbers hackwork."

00:15:18.429 --> 00:15:21.940
In short, most critics' responses were best
summed up in one line:

00:15:21.940 --> 00:15:25.660
"I am very disappointed!"

